In a dank dungeon, lit only by a guttering torch, three men stood over a young woman who knelt on the rush-strewn floor, staring wildly up at them. She was clad only in a scanty shift; her golden hair fell in lustrous ripples over her white shoulders, and her wrists were bound behind her. Even in the uncertain torchlight, despite her disheveled state and pallor of fear, her beauty was striking. She knelt mutely, staring with wide eyes at her tormentors. The men were masked and cloaked—such a deed as this required masks, even in a conquered land. She knew them all nevertheless; but what she knew would harm no one—after that night.  

“Our merciful sovereign offers you one more chance, Countess,” said the tallest of the three, speaking Aquilonian without an accent. “He bids me say that if you soften your proud, rebellious spirit, he will still open his arms to you. If not—” He gestured toward a grim wooden block in the center of the cell. It was blackly stained and deeply notched, as if a keen edge, cutting through some yielding substance, had sunk into the wood.  

Albiona shuddered and turned pale, shrinking back. Every fiber of her vigorous young body quivered with the urge to live. Valerius was young too, and handsome. Many women loved him, she told herself, struggling inwardly for life. But she could not speak the word that would ransom her soft young body from the block and the dripping axe. She could not reason it out; she only knew that when she thought of the clasp of Valerius’ arms, her flesh crawled with an abhorrence greater than the fear of death. She shook her head helplessly, driven by an impulse more irresistible than the instinct to live.

"Then there is no more to be said!" exclaimed one of the others impatiently, speaking with a Nemedian accent. "Where is the headsman?"

As if summoned by the word, the dungeon door opened silently, and a great figure stood framed within it, like a black shadow from the underworld.

Albiona uttered a low, involuntary cry at the sight of that grim shape, and the others stared in silence for a moment, perhaps daunted by superstitious awe at the hooded figure. Through the coif, the eyes blazed like coals of blue fire, and as those eyes rested on each man in turn, a chill crept down his spine.

Then the tall Aquilonian roughly seized the girl and dragged her to the block. She screamed uncontrollably and fought hopelessly against him, frantic with terror, but he forced her to her knees and bent her yellow head down to the bloody block.

"Why do you delay, headsman?" he demanded angrily. "Perform your task!"

He was answered by a short, gusty boom of laughter, indescribably menacing. All in the dungeon froze where they stood, staring at the hooded shape—the two cloaked figures, the masked man bending over the girl, and the girl herself on her knees, twisting her imprisoned head to look upward.

"What means this unseemly mirth, dog?" demanded the Aquilonian uneasily.

The man in black tore the hood from his head and flung it to the ground. He set his back to the closed door and lifted the headsman’s axe.

"Do you know me, dogs?" he rumbled. "Do you know me?"

The breathless silence was broken by a scream.

"The king!" shrieked Albiona, wrenching herself free from the slackened grasp of her captor. "Oh, Mitra, the king!"

The three men stood like statues, then the Aquilonian started and spoke, like one doubting his own senses.

"Conan!" he gasped. "It is the king—or his ghost! What devil’s work is this?"

"Devil’s work to match devils!" mocked Conan, his lips laughing but hell flaming in his eyes. "Come, fall to, my gentlemen. You have your swords, and I this cleaver. no, I think this butcher’s tool suits the work at hand, my fair lords!"

"At him!" muttered the Aquilonian, drawing his sword. "It is Conan, and we must kill or be killed!"

Like men waking from a trance, the Nemedians drew their blades and rushed upon the king.

The headsman’s axe was not made for such work, but Conan wielded the heavy, clumsy weapon as lightly as a hatchet. His quickness of foot, as he constantly shifted position, thwarted their attempt to engage him all three at once.

He caught the first man’s sword on his axe-head and crushed in the wielder’s breast with a murderous counterstroke before the man could step back or parry. The remaining Nemedian, missing a savage swipe, had his brains dashed out before he could recover his balance, and an instant later the Aquilonian was backed into a corner, desperately parrying the crashing strokes that rained upon him, unable even to scream for help.

Suddenly Conan’s long left arm shot out and ripped the mask from the man’s head, revealing pallid features.

"Dog!" grated the king. "I thought I knew you. Traitor! Damned renegade! Even this base steel is too honorable for your foul head. no, die as thieves die!"

The axe fell in a devastating arc, and the Aquilonian cried out, collapsing to his knees, clutching the severed stump of his right arm from which blood spouted. It had been shorn away at the elbow, and the axe, unchecked in its descent, had gashed deeply into his side so that his entrails bulged out.

"Lie there and bleed to death," grunted Conan, casting the axe away in disgust. "Come, Countess!"

Stooping, he slashed the cords that bound her wrists and, lifting her as if she were a child, strode from the dungeon. She was sobbing hysterically, her arms thrown about his corded neck in a frenzied embrace.

"Easy, all," he muttered. "We’re not out of this yet. If we can reach the dungeon where the secret door opens on the stairs that lead to the tunnel—devil take it, they’ve heard that noise, even through these walls."

Down the corridor, arms clanged and the tramp and shouting of men echoed under the vaulted roof. A bent figure came hobbling swiftly along, lantern held high, its light shining full on Conan and the girl. With a curse, the Cimmerian sprang toward him, but the old watchman, abandoning both lantern and pike, scuttled away down the corridor, screeching for help at the top of his cracked voice. Deeper shouts answered him.

Conan turned swiftly and ran the other way. He was cut off from the dungeon with the secret lock and hidden door through which he had entered the Tower and by which he had hoped to leave, but he knew this grim building well. Before he was king, he had been imprisoned in it.

He turned off into a side passage and quickly emerged into another, broader corridor running parallel to the one he had come down, which was at the moment deserted. He followed it only a few yards before turning back down another side passage. This brought him again into the corridor he had left, but at a strategic point. A few feet farther up stood a heavy bolted door, and before it a bearded Nemedian in corselet and helmet, his back to Conan as he peered up the corridor toward the growing tumult and wildly waving lanterns.

Conan did not hesitate. Slipping the girl to the ground, he ran at the guard swiftly and silently, sword in hand. The man turned just as the king reached him, bawled in surprise and fright, and lifted his pike; but before he could bring the clumsy weapon into play, Conan brought his sword down on the fellow’s helmet with a force that would have felled an ox. Helmet and skull gave way together, and the guard crumpled to the floor.

In an instant Conan had drawn the massive bolt that barred the door—too heavy for an ordinary man to move—and called hastily to Albiona, who staggered to him. Catching her up unceremoniously with one arm, he bore her through the door and into the outer darkness.

They had entered a narrow alley, black as pitch, walled by the Tower on one side and the sheer stone backs of a row of buildings on the other. Conan, hurrying through the darkness as swiftly as he dared, felt along the latter wall for doors or windows but found none.

The great door clanged open behind them, and men poured out, torches gleaming on breastplates and naked swords. They glared about, bellowing, unable to pierce the darkness their torches illuminated for only a few feet in any direction, then rushed down the alley at random—heading opposite to the way Conan and Albiona had gone.

“They’ll learn their mistake soon enough,” he muttered, quickening his pace. “If we can find a crack in this infernal wall—damn! The street watch!”

Ahead, a faint glow appeared where the alley opened into a narrow street, and he saw dim figures looming against it, steel glinting faintly. It was indeed the street watch, investigating the noise that had echoed down the alley.

“Who goes there?” they shouted, and Conan ground his teeth at the hated Nemedian accent.

“Keep behind me,” he ordered the girl. “We’ve got to cut our way through before the prison guards come back and trap us between them.”

Grasping his sword, he charged straight at the oncoming figures. The advantage of surprise was his. He could see them, limned against the distant glow, while they could not see him coming out of the black depths of the alley. He was among them before they knew it, striking with the silent fury of a wounded lion.

His only chance lay in hacking through before they could gather their wits. But there were half a score of them, in full mail—hard-bitten veterans of the border wars, whose battle instincts could replace stunned senses. Three were down before they realized only one man was attacking them, but their reaction was instant. The clangor of steel rose deafeningly, sparks flying as Conan’s sword crashed on basinet and hauberk. He could see better than they, and in the dim light his swiftly moving figure was an uncertain mark. Flailing swords cut empty air or glanced from his blade, and when he struck, it was with the fury and certainty of a hurricane.

But behind him came the shouts of the prison guards, returning up the alley at a run, and still the mailed figures before him barred his way with a bristling wall of steel. In an instant the guards would be on his back. In desperation he redoubled his blows, flailing like a smith at his anvil—then suddenly became aware of a diversion. Out of nowhere behind the watchmen rose a score of black figures, and there came the sound of murderously driven blows. Steel glinted in the gloom, and men cried out, struck mortally from behind. In an instant the alley was littered with writhing forms. A dark, cloaked shape sprang toward Conan, who heaved up his sword, catching a gleam of steel in the right hand. But the other hand was extended empty, and a voice hissed urgently, “This way, your Majesty! Quickly!”

With a muttered oath of surprise, Conan caught up Albiona in one massive arm and followed his unknown rescuer. He was not inclined to hesitate, with thirty prison guardsmen closing in behind him.

Surrounded by the mysterious figures, he hurried down the alley, carrying the countess as if she were a child. He could tell nothing of his rescuers except that they wore dark cloaks and hoods. Doubt and suspicion crossed his mind, but they had struck down his enemies, and he saw no better course than to follow.

As if sensing his doubt, the leader touched his arm lightly and said, “Fear not, King Conan; we are your loyal subjects.” The voice was unfamiliar, but the accent was Aquilonian, from the central provinces.

Behind them, the guards were yelling as they stumbled over the bodies in the mud, then came pelting vengefully down the alley, seeing the vague dark mass moving between them and the light of the distant street. But the hooded men turned suddenly toward what seemed a blank wall, and Conan saw a door gape there. He muttered a curse. He had traversed that alley by day, in times past, and had never noticed a door there. But through it they went, and the door closed behind them with the click of a lock. The sound was not reassuring, but his guides hurried him on, moving with the precision of familiarity, guiding Conan with a hand at each elbow. It was like passing through a tunnel, and Conan felt Albiona’s lithe limbs trembling in his arms. Then, somewhere ahead, an opening became faintly visible—a somewhat less black arch in the blackness—and through this they filed.

After that came a bewildering succession of dim courts, shadowy alleys, and winding corridors, all traversed in utter silence, until at last they emerged into a broad, lighted chamber, the location of which Conan could not even guess, for their devious route had confounded even his primitive sense of direction.

10 - A COIN FROM ACHERON  
A Coin from Acheron

Not all his guides entered the chamber. When the door closed, Conan saw only one man standing before him—a slim figure, masked in a black cloak with a hood. The man threw it back, revealing a pale oval face with calm, delicately chiseled features.

The king set Albiona on her feet, but she still clung to him, staring apprehensively about. The chamber was large, with marble walls partly covered by black velvet hangings and thick, rich carpets on the mosaic floor, bathed in the soft golden glow of bronze lamps.

Conan instinctively laid a hand on his hilt. There was blood on his hand, blood clotted about the mouth of his scabbard, for he had sheathed his blade without cleansing it.

“Where are we?” he demanded.

The stranger answered with a low, profound bow in which the suspicious king could detect no trace of irony.

“In the temple of Asura, your Majesty.”

Albiona cried out faintly and clung closer to Conan, staring fearfully at the black arched doors, as if expecting some grisly shape of darkness to enter.

“Fear not, my lady,” said their guide. “There is nothing here to harm you, despite vulgar superstition to the contrary. If your monarch was sufficiently convinced of the innocence of our faith to protect us from the persecution of the ignorant, then surely one of his subjects need have no apprehension.”

"Who are you?" demanded Conan.  

"I am Hadrathus, priest of Asura. One of my followers recognized you when you entered the city and brought word to me."  

Conan grunted profanely.  

"Do not fear that others have discovered your identity," Hadrathus assured him. "Your disguise would have deceived any but a follower of Asura, whose faith teaches us to look beneath the veil of illusion. You were followed to the watchtower, and some of my people entered the tunnel to aid you if you returned that way. Others, myself among them, surrounded the tower. And now, King Conan, it is yours to command. Here in the temple of Asura, you are still king."  

"Why should you risk your lives for me?" asked the king.  

"You were our friend when you sat upon your throne," answered Hadrathus. "You protected us when the priests of Mitra sought to scourge us from the land."  

Conan looked about him curiously. He had never before visited the temple of Asura, nor had he been certain that such a temple even existed in Tarantia. The priests of that faith were known for concealing their sanctuaries with remarkable skill. The worship of Mitra was overwhelmingly predominant in the Hyborian nations, yet the cult of Asura persisted despite official bans and popular hatred. Conan had heard dark tales of hidden temples where thick smoke rose endlessly from black altars, where kidnapped victims were said to be sacrificed before a great coiled serpent whose monstrous head swayed forever in the haunted shadows.  

Persecution had driven the followers of Asura to hide their temples with cunning art and to shroud their rituals in secrecy; and that secrecy, in turn, bred monstrous suspicions and tales of evil.  

But Conan possessed the broad tolerance of the barbarian. He had refused to persecute the followers of Asura or to allow the people to do so on no better evidence than rumor and unproven accusation. "If they are black magicians," he had said, "how will they suffer you to harry them? If they are not, there is no evil in them. Crom’s devils! Let men worship what gods they will."  

At Hadrathus’s respectful invitation, he seated himself on an ivory chair and motioned Albiona to another, but she preferred to sit on a golden stool at his feet, pressing close against his thigh as if seeking safety in his presence. Like most orthodox followers of Mitra, she felt an instinctive horror of the cult of Asura, instilled in her childhood by wild tales of human sacrifice and monstrous gods shambling through shadowed temples.  

Hadrathus stood before them, his uncovered head bowed.  

"What is your wish, your Majesty?"  

"Food first," he grunted, and the priest struck a golden gong with a silver wand.  

Scarcely had the mellow notes faded when four hooded figures entered through a curtained doorway, bearing a great four-legged silver platter laden with steaming dishes and crystal vessels. They set it before Conan, bowed low, and withdrew. The king wiped his hands on the damask and smacked his lips with unconcealed relish.  

"Beware, your Majesty!" whispered Albiona. "These folk eat human flesh!"  

"I’ll stake my kingdom this is nothing but honest roast beef," answered Conan. "Come, lass, eat! You must be hungry after prison fare."  

Thus encouraged, and reassured by the example of one whose word was law to her, the countess complied and ate ravenously though daintily, while her liege lord tore into the meat and guzzled the wine with as much gusto as if he had not already eaten once that night.  

"You priests are shrewd, Hadrathus," he said, holding a great beef bone in his hands and speaking through a mouthful of meat. "I’d welcome your service in my campaign to regain my kingdom."  

Slowly Hadrathus shook his head, and Conan slammed the bone down on the table in a gust of impatient wrath.  

"Crom’s devils! What ails the men of Aquilonia? First Servius—now you! Can you do nothing but wag your idiotic heads when I speak of ousting these dogs?"  

Hadrathus sighed and answered slowly, "My lord, it is ill to say, and I would gladly say otherwise. But the freedom of Aquilonia is at an end. no, the freedom of the whole world may be at an end! Age follows age in the history of the world, and now we enter an age of horror and slavery, as it was long ago."  

"What do you mean?" demanded the king uneasily.  

Hadrathus sank into a chair, resting his elbows on his thighs and staring at the floor.  

"It is not only the rebellious lords of Aquilonia and the armies of Nemedia that are arrayed against you," he said. "It is sorcery—grisly black magic from the grim youth of the world. An awful shape has risen out of the shadows of the past, and none can stand before it."  

"What do you mean?" Conan repeated.  

"I speak of Xaltotun of Acheron, who died three thousand years ago, yet walks the earth today."  

Conan was silent, while in his mind floated an image—the bearded face of calm, inhuman beauty. Again he was haunted by a sense of uneasy familiarity. Acheron—the very sound of the word stirred instinctive echoes of memory and ancient dread.  

"Acheron," he repeated. "Xaltotun of Acheron—man, are you mad? Acheron has been a myth for more centuries than I can remember. I’ve often wondered if it ever existed at all."  

"It was a black reality," answered Hadrathus, "an empire of sorcerers steeped in evil now long forgotten. It was finally overthrown by the Hyborian tribes of the West. The wizards of Acheron practiced foul necromancy—thaumaturgy of the darkest kind—grisly magic taught them by devils. And of all the sorcerers of that accursed kingdom, none was greater than Xaltotun of Python."  

"Then how was he ever overthrown?" asked Conan skeptically.  

"By some means, a source of cosmic power which he jealously guarded was stolen and turned against him. That source has now been returned to him, and he is invincible."  

Albiona, clutching the headsman’s black cloak about her, stared from the priest to the king, not understanding their words. Conan shook his head angrily.

'You are making game of me,' he growled. 'If Xaltotun has been dead three thousand years, how can this man be he? It's some rogue who has taken the old one's name.'

Hadrathus leaned over an ivory table and opened a small gold chest that stood there. From it he took something that glinted dully in the mellow light—a broad gold coin of antique minting.

'You have seen Xaltotun unveiled? Then look upon this. It is a coin stamped in ancient Acheron, before its fall. So pervaded with sorcery was that black empire that even this coin has its uses in the making of magic.'

Conan took it and scowled down at it. There was no mistaking its great antiquity. He had handled many coins in the years of his plundering and had a good practical knowledge of them. The edges were worn and the inscription almost obliterated, but the countenance stamped on one side was still clear-cut and distinct. Conan’s breath hissed between his clenched teeth. It was not cool in the chamber, yet he felt a prickling of his scalp, an icy tightening of his flesh. The countenance was that of a bearded man—inscrutable, with a calm, inhuman beauty.

'By Crom! It's he!' muttered Conan. He understood now the sense of familiarity that the sight of the bearded man had roused in him from the first. He had seen a coin like this once before, long ago in a far land.

With a shake of his shoulders he growled, 'The likeness is only a coincidence—or if he's shrewd enough to assume a forgotten wizard's name, he's shrewd enough to assume his likeness.' But he spoke without conviction. The sight of that coin had shaken the foundations of his universe. He felt that reality and stability were crumbling into an abyss of illusion and sorcery. A wizard was understandable; but this was diabolism beyond sanity.

'We cannot doubt that it is indeed Xaltotun of Python,' said Hadrathus. 'He it was who shook down the cliffs at Valkia by his spells that enthrall the elementals of the earth—he it was who sent the creature of darkness into your tent before dawn.'

Conan scowled at him. 'How did you know that?'

'The followers of Asura have secret channels of knowledge. That does not matter. But do you realize the futility of sacrificing your subjects in a vain attempt to regain your crown?'

Conan rested his chin on his fist and stared grimly into nothing. Albiona watched him anxiously, her mind groping in bewilderment through the maze of the problem that confronted him.

'Is there no wizard in the world who could make magic to fight Xaltotun's magic?' he asked at last.

Hadrathus shook his head. 'If there were, we of Asura would know of him. Men say our cult is a survival of the ancient Stygian serpent-worship. That is a lie. Our ancestors came from Vendhya, beyond the Sea of Vilayet and the blue Himelian mountains. We are sons of the East, not the South, and we have knowledge of all the wizards of the East, who are greater than the wizards of the West. And not one of them would be more than a straw in the wind before the black might of Xaltotun.'

'But he was conquered once,' persisted Conan.

'yes; a cosmic source was turned against him. But now that source is again in his hands, and he will see that it is not stolen again.'

'And what is this damnable source?' demanded Conan irritably.

'It is called the Heart of Ahriman. When Acheron was overthrown, the primitive priest who had stolen it and turned it against Xaltotun hid it in a haunted cavern and built a small temple over the cavern. Thrice thereafter the temple was rebuilt, each time greater and more elaborate than before, but always on the site of the original shrine, though men forgot the reason. Memory of the hidden symbol faded from the minds of common men and was preserved only in priestly books and esoteric volumes. Whence it came no one knows. Some say it is the veritable heart of a god; others that it is a star that fell from the skies long ago. Until it was stolen, none had looked upon it for three thousand years.

'When the magic of the Mitran priests failed against the magic of Xaltotun's acolyte, Altaro, they remembered the ancient legend of the Heart, and the high priest and an acolyte went down into the dark and terrible crypt below the temple, into which no priest had descended for three thousand years. In the ancient iron-bound volumes that speak of the Heart in their cryptic symbolism, it is also told of a creature of darkness left by the ancient priest to guard it.

'Far down in a square chamber with arched doorways leading off into immeasurable blackness, the priest and his acolyte found a black stone altar that glowed dimly with inexplicable radiance.

'On that altar lay a curious gold vessel like a double-valved sea shell that clung to the stone like a barnacle. But it gaped open and empty. The Heart of Ahriman was gone. While they stared in horror, the keeper of the crypt—the creature of darkness—came upon them and mangled the high priest so that he died. But the acolyte fought off the being—a mindless, soulless waif of the pits brought long ago to guard the Heart—and escaped up the long, black, narrow stairs carrying the dying priest, who, before he died, gasped out the news to his followers, bade them submit to a power they could not overcome, and commanded secrecy. But the word has been whispered among the priests, and we of Asura learned of it.'

'And Xaltotun draws his power from this symbol?' asked Conan, still skeptical.

'No. His power is drawn from the black gulf. But the Heart of Ahriman came from some far universe of flaming light, and against it the powers of darkness cannot stand when it is in the hands of an adept. It is like a sword that might smite at him, not a sword with which he can smite. It restores life and can destroy life. He has stolen it not to use against his enemies, but to keep them from using it against him.'

'A shell-shaped bowl of gold on a black altar in a deep cavern,' Conan muttered, frowning as he sought to capture the elusive image. 'That reminds me of something I have heard or seen. But what, in Crom's name, is this notable Heart?'

It was in the form of a great jewel, like a ruby, but pulsing with a blinding fire no ruby had ever known. It glowed like living flame—  

But Conan suddenly sprang up and smote his right fist into his left palm with a thunderclap.  

“Crom!” he roared. “What a fool I’ve been! The Heart of Ahriman! The heart of my kingdom! ‘Find the heart of my kingdom,’ Zelata said. By Ymir, it was the jewel I saw in the green smoke—the jewel Tarascus stole from Xaltotun while he lay in the sleep of the black lotus!”  

Hadrathus was on his feet as well, his calm falling from him like a garment.  

“What are you saying? The Heart stolen from Xaltotun?”  

“yes!” Conan boomed. “Tarascus feared Xaltotun and wanted to cripple his power, which he thought resided in the Heart. Maybe he believed the wizard would die if the Heart were lost. By Crom—ahhh!” With a savage grimace of disappointment and disgust, he dropped his clenched hand to his side.  

“I forgot. Tarascus gave it to a thief to throw into the sea. By this time the fellow must be almost to Kordava. Before I can follow him, he’ll take ship and consign the Heart to the bottom of the ocean.”  

“The sea will not hold it!” exclaimed Hadrathus, quivering with excitement. “Xaltotun would have cast it into the ocean long ago had he not known that the first storm would carry it ashore. But on what unknown beach might it not land!”  

“Well,” Conan said, recovering some of his resilient confidence, “there’s no assurance the thief will throw it away. If I know thieves—and I should, for I was a thief in Zamora in my youth—he won’t throw it away. He’ll sell it to some rich trader. By Crom!” He strode back and forth, his excitement growing. “It’s worth looking for! Zelata bade me find the heart of my kingdom, and all else she showed me proved true. Can it be that the power to conquer Xaltotun lies in that crimson bauble?”  

“yes! My head upon it!” cried Hadrathus, his face alight with fervor, his eyes blazing, his fists clenched. “With it in our hands we can defy the powers of Xaltotun! I swear it! If we can recover it, we have an even chance of reclaiming your crown and driving the invaders from our gates. It is not the swords of Nemedia that Aquilonia fears, but the black arts of Xaltotun.”  

Conan looked at him for a space, impressed by the priest’s fire.  

“It’s like a quest in a nightmare,” he said at last. “Yet your words echo Zelata’s, and all else she said was truth. I’ll seek this jewel.”  

“It holds the destiny of Aquilonia,” said Hadrathus with conviction. “I will send men with you—”  

“no!” exclaimed the king impatiently, unwilling to be hampered by priests on his quest, however skilled in esoteric arts. “This is a task for a fighting man. I go alone. First to Poitain, where I’ll leave Albiona with Trocero. Then to Kordava, and to the sea beyond, if necessary. It may be that even if the thief intends to carry out Tarascus’s order, he’ll have some difficulty finding an outbound ship at this time of year.”  

“And if you find the Heart,” cried Hadrathus, “I will prepare the way for your conquest. Before you return to Aquilonia, I will spread the word through secret channels that you live and are returning with a magic stronger than Xaltotun’s. I will have men ready to rise when you return. They will rise if they are assured they will be protected from Xaltotun’s black arts.  

“And I will aid you on your journey.”  

He rose and struck a gong.  

“A secret tunnel leads from beneath this temple to a place outside the city wall. You shall go to Poitain on a pilgrim’s boat. None will dare molest you.”  

“As you will.” With a definite purpose in mind, Conan was afire with impatience and dynamic energy. “Only let it be done swiftly.”  

